Z||000012||Patrick||07-24-2000||03:47 PM||||<BR>On the way home on the freeway yesterday from the drags at Mission Raceway, I pegged the speedo on my Fiero. And she was still pulling strong in 5th. Not a big deal though, the speedo on my '87 4-banger only goes up to 85 mph... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Batboy, is your water temp gauge reading at the very bottom in your picture?||||reg||14
Z||000013||86SE||07-24-2000||08:47 PM||||Thats a regular auto trany from a v6 Fiero?!?!  At 4200 RPM mine is just barely above 90!  and I think thats after the torque converter locks up, but not sure.||||reg||11
Z||000014||batboy||07-24-2000||10:36 PM||||Bad news, I still have a oil leak, about 1/2 a quart in 100 miles, not good. I guess this motor is coming out before I had planned. Still don't have aluminum heads for the other engine and no money right now anyway. This sucks.<P>Patrick, I'm not sure I understand, are you asking if the temp gauge is the bottom one or are you asking if the temp gauge is reading low. The temp is the upper gauge and the other one is the fuel gauge. Yes the temp gauge reads low, it usually shows 120 degrees normal operating temp, but I've used a calibrated thermometer from work that says it's really 180 degrees. I might have to change gauges or try to recalibrate the needle. One time last summer, it suddenly shot up close to between 180 and 190 on the temp gauge. I immediately stopped and discovered a split radiator hose and low coolant, so it does work...just not accurately.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Patrick||07-25-2000||12:50 AM||||<BR>Yes, I should've worded my inquiry a lot better. I know which gauge is which (I have a Fiero <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), but I was interested in the low reading on your temp gauge. It's confusing to read member's Fiero's temp readings here because most (if not ALL) of the time the readings are reported in Fahrenheit and me being in Canada, our gauges are in Celsius. Mine also reads below the first indicator mark when the engine is warmed up, and I just wondered if this was "normal".||||reg||1
